body:
Thanks for the reply, I was hoping to avoid c++ and just manage it in GDScript.

By good fortunate I discovered (I am a novice to Godot after all) that the skin of a MeshInstance3D could be expanded in the editor, and the indices per bone name shown there (which for the most part were indeed completely different to the Skeleton3D).

By using this as a guide I was able to come up with working GDScript, which may be useful to others:

```
var skel : Skeleton3D = get_parent();

	#Empty Dictionary.
	var bone_skel_to_bind_map = {}

	#Iterate all the binds.
	for i in self.skin.get_bind_count():
		#Get the bind name (bone name) for the current index.
		var bind_index_name = self.skin.get_bind_name(i)

		#Find the Skeleton3D bone index associated with this binds' bone name.
		var skel_bone_index = skel.find_bone(bind_index_name)

		#Add the Skeleton3D Bones Index as the key, the current BindIndex as our value.
		bone_skel_to_bind_map[skel_bone_index] = i;

	#Test the above works - it does!
	var Bone_To_Find = "forearm.L"
	var Bone_To_Find_Skel3D_Index = skel.find_bone(Bone_To_Find);
	var Skin_Bind_Bone_Index = bone_skel_to_bind_map.get(Bone_To_Find_Skel3D_Index);

	#This successfully gets the correct index in the skins' binding for our input bone name.
	print ("Bone: ",Bone_To_Find, " found in Bind Skin at index: ", Skin_Bind_Bone_Index);

	#Test this still works with surface arrays

	#Get the Surface 0 Array.
	var temp = self.mesh.surface_get_arrays(0);

	#Get all the Bones associated with each vert.
	var temp_bones = temp.get(10);

	#For testing, just get the bones associated with vert0 (temp_bones' array index 0-7)
	for i2 in range(0,7):
		#Get the current bone associated with vert0
		var current_bone = temp_bones.get(i2);

		#Inverse our bone map, use the current bone (which is the dictionary value)
		#to find our Skeleton3D's associated bone name (dictionary key).
		var Skel3DIndex = bone_skel_to_bind_map.find_key(current_bone)

		#This works, the correct name is returned.
		print(skel.get_bone_name(Skel3DIndex))
```
